    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d353">

                <pb facs="0343" n="331" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg331"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/15442: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Earle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-09-27T12:00:00+02:00">September 27,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 4:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>402. The Secretary General of the Foreign Office this morning protested orally to
                    the Legation on behalf of his Government against articles he alleges have
                    recently appeared in the American press according to which: <list>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>)</label>
                        <item>Bulgaria has agreed to take aggressive action against Russia.</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>)</label>
                        <item>In return Germany will give Bulgaria a protectorate over the Crimea
                            and a dominant position in the Black Sea.</item>
                        <label>(<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>)</label>
                        <item>There is widespread unrest in Bulgaria and the Government is summarily
                            executing Communists, including Communist deputies.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0344" n="332" xml:id="pg332"/>
                <p>The Secretary General stated that such articles are worse than anything which has
                    been appearing in the Bulgarian press against the United States (see my 394,
                    September 24).</p>
                <p>It was explained to the Secretary General that the American press is not
                    government controlled and consequently the American Government cannot be held
                    responsible for statements made therein. However, I said I would be glad to
                    report his denial to my Government.</p>
                <p>In my opinion point (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) seems very unlikely. As for
                    point (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>), I do not think Bulgaria has at the
                    moment any aggressive intentions against Russia, while I cannot conscientiously
                    say there is, in point (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>), no element of
                    truth.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Earle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
